It depends a lot on what you're testing.

I've had the opposite experience in the past. I used to be an evangelist for 100% test coverage (I still write unit tests religiously, and the apps I write are usually at 100% coverage) but I've found that when you're writing code that's intended to be a platform for other people to build on top of, 100% test coverage can actually be dangerous.

I can't say that's always true, but if you're building a platform, it can be dangerous because you can end up in a situation where you're testing an API that no one uses at all.

For example, I wrote a class that represented a collection of things. This class had a method, Count, that returned the number of things in the collection. And there was a test that tested that the Count method worked correctly. It also had a method called CountNonEmpty, which returned the number of non-empty things in the collection. This method was not tested, because I had never written any code that used it.

In practice, it turns out that CountNonEmpty is a terrible idea, because it's very inefficient. The correct way to implement it would be:

```csharp
public int CountNonEmpty()
{
    int count = 0;
    foreach (var thing in this)
        if (!thing.IsEmpty()) count++;
    return count;
}
```

But it turns out that if you implement it this way, it takes forever to run, because you end up loading all the objects in the collection just to count them. So you really should implement it as:

```csharp
public int CountNonEmpty()
{
    return this.Where(t => !t.IsEmpty()).Count();
}
```

This second implementation is much more efficient, because it does the counting on the server, rather than loading the objects on the client and counting them there.

But I didn't test the first implementation, because I had never written code that used the CountNonEmpty function, so I never noticed that it was broken. And in fact, I think the first implementation may have been broken at the time I wrote it.

I only found out that the first implementation was broken by accident. I got an email from someone who had written code that used the CountNonEmpty function, and they told me that they thought it had a bug in it.

If I'd been testing the CountNonEmpty function, I'd have noticed the bug much earlier. But I didn't, because I wasn't using the function in my own code.

I think the moral of the story is that you need to be very careful about how you do unit testing. I still do unit tests religiously, but I also try to keep track of which of my functions are tested and which are not, because it's easy to miss important tests.

I’ve been writing software for about 22 years now, and I’m now pretty sure the vast majority of code comments I ever see are apologizing for something, or explaining why the code is as ugly as it is. This has become an important heuristic for me in code review.

For example, let’s say I have this code:

```
if (a < b && b < c && c > 0 && c != 0) {
    doSomething();
}
```

and I review it, my first reaction is likely to be “oh man, the conditions look redundant.” So I’ll be surprised and delighted when I see a comment like this:

```
if (a < b && b < c && c > 0 && c != 0) {
    // c > 0 is redundant if b < c and b > 0, which is what a < b
    // and b < c means, but the compiler can't see that, and it's
    // faster to have a single comparison instead of multiple, so
    // we do it here.
    doSomething();
}
```

I’ll be delighted because this comment is explaining why the code is the way it is. The code looks redundant in a way that isn’t, and the comment explains why that’s the case.

The vast majority of comments I see are of this kind. I can think of a few kinds of comments that aren’t, though.

One is documentation comments. Those don’t really explain why the code is the way it is. For example, you’ll see code like this

```
// Add two integers together.
int sum(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

which doesn’t explain why the code is the way it is. But there are a few kinds of documentation comments that do explain the code. For example:

```
// Add two integers together. This could be implemented with a
// table lookup, but then it would require 1024 bytes of memory
// and a couple of cycles. Here we use addition because it's
// faster and uses less memory.
int sum(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

Another case is comments explaining algorithms. For example, let’s say we have a comment like this:

```
// We want to sum all the numbers in an array. We'll do it like
// this:
// * First make a copy of the array.
// * Then loop over the array and add each element to the sum.
// * Finally return the sum.
int sum(int *arr, int len) {
    int tmp[len];
    memcpy(tmp, arr, sizeof(int) * len);
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        sum += tmp[i];
    }
    return sum;
}
```

This kind of comment also doesn’t explain why the code is the way it is, but it is still useful.

Another kind of comment that doesn’t explain why the code is the way it is is one that explains why a variable has a particular value, like this:

```
const MAX = 1024; // because that's the maximum size of a frame in a network packet.
```

These are pretty rare, but they do exist.

The most common kind of comment that doesn’t explain why the code is the way it is, though, is the comment that explains that the code is doing something obvious. For example:

```
// Check if the number is positive.
if (n > 0) {
    doSomething();
}
```

This doesn’t explain why the code is the way it is, but it does explain what the code is doing, which might be useful if the comment is in a different language than the code. For example:

```
// Comprueba si el número es positivo.
if (n > 0) {
    doSomething();
}
```

The reason this kind of comment is so rare is that it’s really obvious what the code is doing in the first place, and it takes up space. It’s not the kind of comment I would write, but I can imagine it being useful in a project where a lot of the developers don’t know English, and they’re all working on the same code base.

And then there are the comments that apologize for something, like this:

```
if (n > 0 && n < 10 && n > 0 && n < 10) {
    // the second pair of comparisons is redundant, but I left it
    // in for now because it was there when I inherited this code
    // and I didn't want to break it.
    doSomething();
}
```

This kind of comment is really useful to have around.

There are a few other kinds of comments I’ve seen that I haven’t mentioned here, but I think that covers most of them.

I wrote an article on it last week: https://overreacted.io/a-complete-guide-to-useeffect/

Short version: use useEffect to sync a React component with something outside React, like fetching data or setting the title of the document.

A complete guide to useEffect:

The useEffect Hook lets you synchronize a component with some external system.

The term “external system” is broad and it might not be immediately clear why this is the right mental model, so let’s start with a simple example.

Imagine you work on a web app and you want to change the document title based on the current data. For example, when you are looking at a specific conversation, you might want the document title to be the name of the other person in that conversation.

This sounds easy, but it’s actually one of the trickier problems in React!

The tricky part

Here is our component. We know how to set the title on initial load, but how do we update the title when the data changes?

```
function Chat({ contact, messages }) {
  document.title = contact.name;
  return <MessageList messages={messages} />;
}
```

It’s tempting to put this code inside the component, but it’s too late!

React renders components inside a special “rendering” mode where we are not supposed to modify external systems. This is important because rendering is a special mode that might happen many times for the same component.

For example, let’s say that contact.name changes in the parent, but messages don’t. React might decide to not re-render our component because it didn’t change, or it might have the component “remember” its previously rendered output.

In other words, if we put code like document.title = contact.name; inside the component, it might not run when we want it to.

This is a problem we often need to solve for other kinds of external systems.

The useEffect solution

Let’s introduce the useEffect Hook. It lets us “escape” from the rendering mode and into an “effects” mode where we can do things like changing the title.

The name useEffect might sound scary, but this is a pretty simple concept. It just means “doing things”.

Here is how you would write the same idea with useEffect:

```
import { useEffect } from 'react';

function Chat({ contact, messages }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    document.title = contact.name;
  });

  return <MessageList messages={messages} />;
}
```

When React renders a component, it will queue up any useEffects it encounters. Then, after the browser has updated the screen with the UI changes, it will run these effects in the order they were queued.

In other words, effects don’t run during rendering, but after the screen updates.

This is a good rule of thumb for deciding where to put code: if you want to change the UI, you do it during rendering. If you want to “do something”, like set the title of the document, you do it in an effect.

The useEffect code can also return a function to “clean up” after itself. Here is how we would change our code to use this:

```
import { useEffect } from 'react';

function Chat({ contact, messages }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    const previousTitle = document.title;
    document.title = contact.name;
    return () => {
      document.title = previousTitle;
    };
  });
  return <MessageList messages={messages} />;
}
```

If we did not use the return value to clean up, then when this component unmounts, the title would remain set to the contact’s name. Now it goes back to whatever it was before.

Effects don’t run on every render

The reason we needed useEffect to set document.title is that we need to run this code when the data changes.

It’s tempting to think that we should do this in the component body, but as we saw above, that code might not run when we want it to.

For example, let’s say the name changes to “Taylor Swift”:

```
const contact = { name: 'Taylor Swift' };
ReactDOM.render(<Chat contact={contact} />);
```

React will render our component, and after the screen is updated, it will run the effect. We will end up with the right title.

However, if the contact object is the same, React might not run our component.

```
ReactDOM.render(<Chat contact={contact} />);
```

In that case, we don’t need to set the title again anyway!

So effects only run when we need them to.

This is what we call a “synchronization” between the component and something external, like the title of the document.

Effects can be declared conditionally

If we wanted to only set the title if the contact was not null, we could conditionally declare this effect:

```
function Chat({ contact, messages }) {
  if (contact !== null) {
    useEffect(() => {
      const previousTitle = document.title;
      document.title = contact.name;
      return () => {
        document.title = previousTitle;
      };
    });
  }
  return <MessageList messages={messages} />;
}
```

You might have seen that you can’t call conditional hooks in React, but it’s okay to conditionally declare effects!

This is because we are not changing the number of hooks in our component. There is always one hook, and it just happens that we are not declaring an effect when it’s not necessary.

This is a great rule of thumb for thinking about when to declare an effect. We only need to declare it when we want to perform a side effect.

For example, when we want to set the title of the document.

What’s an “effect”?

It’s important to understand what an effect is, so let’s start from the beginning.

An effect is when your code has some kind of consequence outside your own program.

For example, this is not an effect because it doesn’t do anything externally:

```
const age = 42;
```

But this is an effect:

```
console.log('I am 42');
```

This is an effect too:

```
document.title = 'Taylor Swift';
```

The term “side effect” refers to the fact that these things change something about the world outside your program.

An effect is the part of your program that interacts with the outside world.

An “effect” is not the same as an “event handler”

I’ve seen people think of useEffect as “event listeners”, but I think that’s a misleading mental model.

Event listeners are one kind of effect, but an effect doesn’t have to be an event listener. For example, this is also an effect:

```
fetch('https://example.com');
```

Effects are what we do when we “interact with the outside world” — like logging something, updating the page title, or fetching data.

Event handlers are one way in which effects can happen, but there are other ways, like with useEffect.

This is not a complete list of effects

I don’t want this article to become a “list of useEffect examples”, so I’m going to try to end on a more general note.

If you’re asking yourself “is this something I can do in useEffect?”, the answer is usually “yes”.

If your code is doing something to interact with an external system, you can do it in useEffect.

Here are a few examples:

```
// Logging is an effect.
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('Hello');
});

// Updating the title is an effect.
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = 'Taylor Swift';
});

// Fetching is an effect.
useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/user');
});

// Subscribing to an event is an effect.
useEffect(() => {
  const listener = () => {};
  document.addEventListener('click', listener);
  return () => {
    document.removeEventListener('click', listener);
  };
});
```

The useEffect Hook is the only React Hook that lets you do this, and it’s the only way to interact with external systems in a declarative way.

You can’t use useEffect for rendering

A good rule of thumb is that useEffect is a good place to do anything that is not a render.

For example, you should not use useEffect to update the UI. If you want to update the UI, you need to do it during rendering.

You should not use it to set state. If you want to set state, you can just set it during rendering.

If you want to fetch data, you can’t use useEffect to fetch it and then immediately display it to the user. That would be too late. Instead, we need to fetch the data before rendering the UI.

This is why I like to think of effects as a way to escape from the rendering world and interact with the outside world.

Effects are a useful concept that you can learn about even if you don’t know React. If you like, you can learn more about them in the React docs.

The idea behind "synchronizing with external systems" is that the DOM isn't the only thing you might need to keep in sync with your app. For example, you might need to keep a database, a WebSocket connection, or the page title in sync with your UI.

If you think of React as being responsible for rendering to a DOM, then useEffect is a general-purpose way to render to other systems. For example, you can update a page title with useEffect.

There's an important detail to note: the "external system" can just be the browser. For example, the browser maintains the state of which element has focus, which is outside of React's control. So if you want to focus an element when a user clicks a button, you can do that in an effect.

```jsx
function HighlightedButton() {
  const buttonRef = useRef(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    buttonRef.current.focus();
  }, []);
  return <button ref={buttonRef} />;
}
```

In this case, the "external system" you're syncing with is the browser's own notion of which element is focused.

It's common to use this technique to set up subscriptions to external systems, like a WebSocket connection. To do that, you first create the subscription in the effect, and then return a cleanup function from the effect to remove the subscription when the component unmounts.

```js
function FriendStatus({ userId }) {
  const [isOnline, setIsOnline] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => {
    function handleOnline() {
      setIsOnline(true);
    }
    function handleOffline() {
      setIsOnline(false);
    }
    const friend = new Friend(userId);
    friend.add('online', handleOnline);
    friend.add('offline', handleOffline);
    return () => {
      friend.remove('online', handleOnline);
      friend.remove('offline', handleOffline);
    };
  }, [userId]);
  // ...
}
```

The cleanup function here is a lot like an effect's destructor: when the component unmounts, it cleans up the subscription.

Think of render as computing the next state of the UI, and useEffect as doing something *after* React has updated the page, and only doing it once. Effects are for *imperative* code that is not part of the React declarative model.

A classic example is subscribing to something:

```js
function ChatMessage({ roomId }) {
  const [room, setRoom] = useState();

  useEffect(() => {
    const room = getRoom(roomId);
    room.subscribe(setRoom);
    return () => {
      room.unsubscribe(setRoom);
    };
  }, [roomId]);

  return room.name;
}
```

This is not declarative, because it is imperative code. You can't write this in a declarative way.

When you use an effect, you should write it so that the effect can run any time, and that's ok. It will have no effect because nothing has changed.

Caches are a memory optimization that tries to use the locality of the memory access pattern to its advantage.

The idea is that the L1 cache is very fast, but also small, and that the L2 cache is slower but larger. The L1 cache will hold data from a small area of memory, and if you read from memory near that area you get a hit in the L1 cache instead of a miss. This is called temporal locality.

So if you have a program like:

```
void read_all() {
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        cacheable_read(&global_array[i]);
}
```

that reads all of global_array, you can expect that reading a given global_array[i] will often be a cache hit, because the previous global_array[i-1] read will have already brought it into the cache.

This is a good example of spatial locality: when you read global_array[i-1], you read a whole cache line of 64 bytes, and global_array[i] will also be in that cache line, so the next read will hit in the cache.

However, if you do

```
void read_every_other() {
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i += 2)
        cacheable_read(&global_array[i]);
}
```

you might not get a cache hit, because when you read global_array[i], the cache line that you load might only go up to global_array[i + 7], and by the time you get around to reading global_array[i + 2] that cache line might have been evicted, and you'll have to load it again from memory. This is an example of spatial anti-locality.

When I talk about cache-friendly data structures I mean that they take advantage of temporal and spatial locality.

So for example, if I have a linked list, when I traverse it I'm reading from memory in a sort of random way, so I'll get a cache miss on each node, which is not great. If I store the linked list nodes in an array, then at least they'll be close to each other, and I'll get cache hits on most nodes.

You shouldn't throw exceptions to implement loops, as in

```python
while True:
    try:
        do_stuff()
    except Done:
        break
```

StopIteration is different: it's part of the protocol for iterators. The while loop is still the control flow.

The loop borrows the Vec immutably, and the push borrows the whole struct mutably, which includes that Vec. You can't hold both, so the compiler refuses. Collect the new items into a temporary instead, then move it into place after the loop:

```rust
struct Pair {
    evens: Vec<i32>,
    out: Vec<i32>,
}

impl Pair {
    fn double_evens(&mut self) {
        let mut doubled = Vec::with_capacity(self.evens.len());
        for n in &self.evens {
            if n % 2 == 0 {
                doubled.push(2 * n);
            }
        }
        self.out = doubled;
    }
}
```

The temporary borrows nothing, so assigning it at the end is fine.

The loop holds an immutable borrow of the Vec for its whole duration, so while it runs you cannot mutably borrow anything that the Vec is part of. The usual fix is to take what you need out of the immutable borrow before mutating:

```rust
struct S {
    items: Vec<u32>,
    log: Vec<u32>,
}

impl S {
    fn double_into_log(&mut self) {
        let doubled: Vec<u32> = self.items.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();
        self.log.extend(doubled);
    }
}
```

If the iterator yields owned values you don't need to collect first: `for x in self.items.drain(..)` gives you each element by value and you can push while draining.

An iterator borrows its collection, so `for x in self.items` holds `&self.items` for the whole loop, which is incompatible with `&mut self.out`. The usual fix is to iterate over indices, which borrows only the elements:

```rust
for i in 0..self.items.len() {
    let x = self.items[i].clone();
    self.out.push(x);
}
```

Or iterate over a temporary that owns the values: `for x in std::mem::take(&mut self.items) { ... }` empties `items` and leaves it empty afterward.
